#
# The number of `interfacebloat` findings tolerated in the tree. This is a
# RATCHET: CI fails if the count goes UP, and also fails if it goes DOWN without
# this number being lowered in the same PR. Both directions are deliberate --
# a baseline nobody lowers is a baseline that stops meaning anything.
#
# Why a count and not a per-file list: golangci-lint's result cache is keyed by
# file content, so an unchanged file replays whichever PATH was recorded first,
# which for this repo meant paths pointing into sibling worktrees. A per-file
# baseline would have been built on the unstable half of the output.
#
# CORRECTION 2026-08-18: this note used to add that the count itself was stable
# across checkouts and caches, citing 28 from the repo root, 28 from inside a
# worktree and 28 from an isolated clone. That measurement predated the first
# //nolint:interfacebloat override, and the stability did not survive it. A
# replayed path breaks BOTH things that read it -- suppression, which re-reads
# the source at that position, and .golangci.yml's `\.worktrees/` exclusion. So
# the count moved in both directions: 6 instead of 4 from a worktree whose
# sibling had been deleted, and 0 instead of 4 from the repo root with a sibling
# present. The 0 is the one to fear, because this gate then advises lowering the
# baseline to it. scripts/check-interface-width.sh scopes GOLANGCI_LINT_CACHE per
# worktree so neither is reachable; see that file and PR #2564.
#
# The count is still the right thing to ratchet on -- but because the paths are
# unstable, not because the count was ever immune.
#
# Two ways to go over, both of which leave a reviewable trace:
#   1. Per interface -- `//nolint:interfacebloat // <reason>`. nolintlint is
#      configured with require-explanation and require-specific, so a silent or
#      unexplained override does not compile past the linter.
#   2. Wholesale -- raise the number below. That is a one-line diff in a file
#      whose only purpose is to hold this number, so it cannot land unnoticed.
#
# Baseline set 2026-08-18 with a cleared golangci-lint cache, after the split
# sweep took the count from 28 to 5 (#2542, #2545, #2546, #2547, #2549, #2550,
# #2553, #2554, #2556), then to 4 by deleting audiobookUpdateStore -- a second
# declaration of audiobookStore under another name, whose own direct-call set
# was the single method GetBookByID.
#
# Lowered to 3 by narrowing itunesservice.Store from 17 database.* embeds to the
# seven interfaces its consumers were measured to need. That one came down only
# after its six subsystem leaves did (#2560, #2561, #2563): the aggregate was
# wide because each subsystem held it rather than its own slice, so it could not
# be argued down on its own terms.
#
# The three held here are documented in
# docs/audits/2026-08-18-interface-width-shapes.md section 6 -- audiobookStore
# (11), database.Store (40) and maintenance.JobStore (12). Each is genuinely wide
# rather than badly grouped: audiobookStore needs the service behind it split,
# database.Store is the union type and the plan is to make it unreachable rather
# than smaller, and JobStore's width was the deliberate outcome of the #2534
# arbitration. So this number is expected to hold until those decisions are
# taken, not to keep ticking down.
3
